.\"	@(#)putc.3s	6.2 (Berkeley) 11/6/85
.\"
.TH PUTC 3S  "August 1, 1992"
.ds ]W ANSI C
.SH NAME
putc, putchar, fputc \- put character on a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int putc(int \fIc\fP, FILE *\fIstream\fP);
.PP
.B int putchar(int \fIc\fP);
.PP
.B int fputc(int \fIc\fP, FILE *\fIstream\fP);
.PP
.B int putw(int \fIw\fP, FILE *\fIstream\fP);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Putc
appends the character
.I c
(converted to an unsigned char)
to the named output
.IR stream .
It returns the character written.
.PP
.IR Putchar ( c )
is defined as 
.IR putc ( c ,
.BR stdout ).
.PP
.I Fputc
behaves like 
.IR putc ,
but is a genuine function rather than a macro.
.PP
.I Putw
appends word
(that is, an
.BR int )
.I w
to the output
.IR stream .
It returns the word written.
.I Putw
neither assumes nor causes special alignment in the file.
.I Putw
is 
neither ANSI- nor POSIX-compliant.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
These functions return the character written.
If a write error occurs,
the error indicator for the stream is set,
.I errno
is set, and
.SM
.B EOF
is returned.
Since
.SM
.B EOF
is a valid integer,
.IR ferror (3S)
should be used to detect 
.I putw
errors.
.SH ERRORS
The underlying function of these functions
is \fIwrite\fR(2).  The error conditions specified for \fIread\fR(2)
apply to 
them.
.SH BUGS
Because it is implemented as a macro,
.I putc
treats a
.I stream
argument with side effects improperly.  In particular
\*(lqputc(c, *f++)\*(rq
.PP
doesn't work sensibly.
.PP
Errors can occur long after the call to
.IR putc .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
fopen(3S),
fclose(3S),
getc(3S),
puts(3S),
printf(3S),
fread(3S)
